{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18169","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18169","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18169","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Purui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Purui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengbo","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Hengbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingzhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guannan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Guannan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PiERN: Token-Level Routing for Integrating High-Precision Computation and Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-17T10:15:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:01:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-27T06:44:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:26:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T12:46:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T02:08:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T09:14:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:54:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tasks on complex systems require high-precision numerical computation to support decisions. However, current large language models (LLMs), even with enhanced reasoning capabilities, cannot integrate such computations as an intrinsic and interpretable capability with existing architectures. To this end, we propose Physically-isolated Experts Routing Network (PiERN), an architecture that directs computation and reasoning at token level, thereby enabling iterative alternation within a single chain of thought. We systematically evaluate PiERN on representative computation-reasoning tasks, including PDEBench and battery management tasks. Results show that PiERN achieves not only higher accuracy than directly finetuning LLMs but also significant improvements in response latency, token usage, GPU energy consumption, and experts routing accuracy compared with mainstream multi-agent approaches, while exhibiting no significant degradation in performance on MMLU and GLUE benchmarks. PiERN offers an efficient, interpretable, and scalable paradigm for interfacing language models with scientific systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18169","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-24T01:51:55Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:51:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:15:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18127","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18127","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18127","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Weng","name":"Weng, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ej","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinqin","familyName":"He","name":"He, Qinqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialing","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Jialing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixuan","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Zhixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiting","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Safe-SAIL: Towards a Fine-grained Safety Landscape of Large Language Models via Sparse Autoencoder Interpretation Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T11:22:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:00:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T03:58:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T22:23:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T10:00:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-20T00:23:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.944","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) enable interpretability research by decomposing entangled model activations into monosemantic features. However, under what circumstances SAEs derive most fine-grained latent features for safety, a low-frequency concept domain, remains unexplored. Two key challenges exist: identifying SAEs with the greatest potential for generating safety domain-specific features, and the prohibitively high cost of detailed feature explanation. In this paper, we propose Safe-SAIL, a unified framework for interpreting SAE features in safety-critical domains to advance mechanistic understanding of large language models. Safe-SAIL introduces a pre-explanation evaluation metric to efficiently identify SAEs with strong safety domain-specific interpretability, and reduces interpretation cost by 55% through a segment-level simulation strategy. Building on Safe-SAIL, we train a comprehensive suite of SAEs with human-readable explanations and systematic evaluations for 1,758 safety-related features spanning four domains: pornography, politics, violence, and terror. Using this resource, we conduct empirical analyses and provide insights on the effectiveness of Safe-SAIL for risk feature identification and how safety-critical entities and concepts are encoded across model layers. All models, explanations, and tools are publicly released in our open-source toolkit and companion product."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18127","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-24T01:50:55Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:50:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-20T02:22:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18010","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18010","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18010","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Papi","name":"Papi, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis","familyName":"Fucci","name":"Fucci, Dennis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Gaido","name":"Gaido, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Negri","name":"Negri, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luisa","familyName":"Bentivogli","name":"Bentivogli, Luisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Attention is Half Explanation in Speech-to-Text Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:49:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:47:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T14:35:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T02:44:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cross-attention is a core mechanism in encoder-decoder architectures, widespread in many fields, including speech-to-text (S2T) processing. Its scores have been repurposed for various downstream applications--such as timestamp estimation and audio-text alignment--under the assumption that they reflect the dependencies between input speech representation and the generated text. While the explanatory nature of attention mechanisms has been widely debated in the broader NLP literature, this assumption remains largely unexplored within the speech domain. To address this gap, we assess the explanatory power of cross-attention in S2T models by comparing its scores to input saliency maps derived from feature attribution. Our analysis spans monolingual and multilingual, single-task and multi-task models at multiple scales, and shows that attention scores moderately to strongly align with saliency-based explanations, particularly when aggregated across heads and layers. However, it also shows that cross-attention captures only about 50% of the input relevance and, in the best case, only partially reflects how the decoder attends to the encoder's representations--accounting for just 52-75% of the saliency. These findings uncover fundamental limitations in interpreting cross-attention as an explanatory proxy, suggesting that it offers an informative yet incomplete view of the factors driving predictions in S2T models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at INTERSPEECH 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18010","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:25:29Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:25:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:34:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18158","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18158","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18158","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungyoun","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Seungyoun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minsoo","familyName":"Khang","name":"Khang, Minsoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungrae","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sungrae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ZERA: Zero-init Instruction Evolving Refinement Agent -- From Zero Instructions to Structured Prompts via Principle-based Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-17T01:47:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T23:24:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automatic Prompt Optimization (APO) improves large language model (LLM) performance by refining prompts for specific tasks. However, prior APO methods typically focus only on user prompts, rely on unstructured feedback, and require large sample sizes and long iteration cycles-making them costly and brittle. We propose ZERA (Zero-init Instruction Evolving Refinement Agent), a novel framework that jointly optimizes both system and user prompts through principled, low-overhead refinement. ZERA scores prompts using eight generalizable criteria with automatically inferred weights, and revises prompts based on these structured critiques. This enables fast convergence to high-quality prompts using minimal examples and short iteration cycles. We evaluate ZERA across five LLMs and nine diverse datasets spanning reasoning, summarization, and code generation tasks. Experimental results demonstrate consistent improvements over strong baselines. Further ablation studies highlight the contribution of each component to more effective prompt construction. Our implementation including all prompts is publicly available at https://github.com/younatics/zera-agent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures. To appear in EMNLP 2025 Main Conference (Oral Presentation)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18158","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-24T01:51:40Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:51:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-29T02:50:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Hongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Matos","name":"Matos, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hari Krishna","familyName":"Gadi","name":"Gadi, Hari Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niclas","familyName":"Zeller","name":"Zeller, Niclas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Cremers","name":"Cremers, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liqiu","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Liqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TurnBack: A Geospatial Route Cognition Benchmark for Large Language Models through Reverse Route"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-17T15:00:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:01:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Humans can interpret geospatial information through natural language, while the geospatial cognition capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) remain underexplored. Prior research in this domain has been constrained by non-quantifiable metrics, limited evaluation datasets and unclear research hierarchies. Therefore, we propose a large-scale benchmark and conduct a comprehensive evaluation of the geospatial route cognition of LLMs. We create a large-scale evaluation dataset comprised of 36000 routes from 12 metropolises worldwide. Then, we introduce PathBuilder, a novel tool for converting natural language instructions into navigation routes, and vice versa, bridging the gap between geospatial information and natural language. Finally, we propose a new evaluation framework and metrics to rigorously assess 11 state-of-the-art (SOTA) LLMs on the task of route reversal. The benchmark reveals that LLMs exhibit limitation to reverse routes: most reverse routes neither return to the starting point nor are similar to the optimal route. Additionally, LLMs face challenges such as low robustness in route generation and high confidence for their incorrect answers. Code\\ \\\u0026amp;\\ Data available here: \\href{https://github.com/bghjmn32/EMNLP2025_Turnback}{TurnBack.}"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 (Main). This is the camera-ready/author version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-24T01:52:00Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:52:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T01:52:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junlin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zehao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaowei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Shaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanlan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SIRAG: Towards Stable and Interpretable RAG with A Process-Supervised Multi-Agent Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-17T09:09:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:01:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enables large language models (LLMs) to access external knowledge sources, but the effectiveness of RAG relies on the coordination between the retriever and the generator. Since these components are developed independently, their interaction is often suboptimal: the retriever may return irrelevant or redundant documents, while the generator may fail to fully leverage retrieved evidence. In this work, we propose a process-supervised multi-agent framework to bridge the gap between retriever and generator. The framework introduces two lightweight agents: a Decision Maker, which determines when to continue retrieval or stop for answer generation, and a Knowledge Selector, which filters retrieved documents to retain only the most useful evidence. To provide fine-grained supervision, we employ an LLM-as-a-Judge that evaluates each intermediate action with process-level rewards, ensuring more accurate credit assignment than relying solely on final answer correctness. We further adopt a tree-structured rollout strategy to explore diverse reasoning paths, and train both agents with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) in an end-to-end manner. Experiments on single-hop and multi-hop question answering benchmarks show that our approach achieves higher accuracy, more stable convergence, and produces more interpretable reasoning trajectories compared with standard RAG baselines. Importantly, the proposed framework is modular and plug-and-play, requiring no modification to the retriever or generator, making it practical for real-world RAG applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages,2 figures, IRAC under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-24T01:51:52Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:51:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T01:51:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18163","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18163","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18163","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haodong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Haodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Chenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yansi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yansi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuosheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhuosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongshen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Gongshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thinking in a Crowd: How Auxiliary Information Shapes LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-17T06:45:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:01:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The capacity of Large Language Models (LLMs) to reason is fundamental to their application in complex, knowledge-intensive domains. In real-world scenarios, LLMs are often augmented with external information that can be helpful, irrelevant, or even misleading. This paper investigates the causal impact of such auxiliary information on the reasoning process of LLMs with explicit step-by-step thinking capabilities. We introduce SciAux, a new dataset derived from ScienceQA, to systematically test the robustness of the model against these types of information. Our findings reveal a critical vulnerability: the model's deliberative \"thinking mode\" is a double-edged sword. While helpful context improves accuracy, misleading information causes a catastrophic drop in performance, which is amplified by the thinking process. Instead of conferring robustness, thinking reinforces the degree of error when provided with misinformation. This highlights that the challenge is not merely to make models \"think\", but to endow them with the critical faculty to evaluate the information upon which their reasoning is based. The SciAux dataset is available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/billhdzhao/SciAux."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18163","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-24T01:51:47Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:51:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T01:51:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18113","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18113","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18113","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanzi","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Guanzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianze","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Tianze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Heyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Prompt Fusion for Multi-Task and Cross-Domain Adaptation in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T23:42:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study addresses the generalization limitations commonly observed in large language models under multi-task and cross-domain settings. Unlike prior methods such as SPoT, which depends on fixed prompt templates, our study introduces a unified multi-task learning framework with dynamic prompt scheduling mechanism. By introducing a prompt pool and a task-aware scheduling strategy, the method dynamically combines and aligns prompts for different tasks. This enhances the model's ability to capture semantic differences across tasks. During prompt fusion, the model uses task embeddings and a gating mechanism to finely control the prompt signals. This ensures alignment between prompt content and task-specific demands. At the same time, it builds flexible sharing pathways across tasks. In addition, the proposed optimization objective centers on joint multi-task learning. It incorporates an automatic learning strategy for scheduling weights, which effectively mitigates task interference and negative transfer. To evaluate the effectiveness of the method, a series of sensitivity experiments were conducted. These experiments examined the impact of prompt temperature parameters and task number variation. The results confirm the advantages of the proposed mechanism in maintaining model stability and enhancing transferability. Experimental findings show that the prompt scheduling method significantly improves performance on a range of language understanding and knowledge reasoning tasks. These results fully demonstrate its applicability and effectiveness in unified multi-task modeling and cross-domain adaptation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18113","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-24T01:50:36Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:50:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T01:50:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":8,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=%28doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18010+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18113+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18127+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18158+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18163+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18167+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18169+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18173%29"}}